{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And unto half the tribe of Manasseh the son of Joseph</b> – At first the tribe of Manasseh did not come before him, but when he divided the land to the two tribes, he saw that it was a territory larger than what was fitting for them, and he sought whoever wished to take an inheritance with them. And there were men of the tribe of Manasseh who desired it — perhaps they were the men of Machpeh — and he gave them their portion. The sense of <b>and unto half</b> is one portion of them, as in \"then the people of Israel were split in half: half the people were after Tivni son of Ginath to make him king, and half after Omri\" (I Kings 16:21). The matter is that the children of Manasseh were eight ancestral houses, as written in the portion of the census (above 26:29–32), and the family of the Machirites and the family of the Gileadites inherited in this land, for they were men of valor and captured it for themselves, and he gave them a large portion of their number. And the six families crossed the Jordan, as it is written in Joshua (17:2): \"And unto the children of Manasseh who remained, according to their families — unto the children of Abiezer, and unto the children of Helek, and unto the children of Asriel, and unto the children of Shechem, and unto the children of Hepher, and unto the children of Shemida.\" And this is what is written there (verse 5): \"And there fell ten portions to Manasseh, besides the land of Gilead and Bashan, which is beyond the Jordan.\" The reason is that they made of the land nine equal portions for the nine tribes, and they could not give the remaining tribe of Manasseh a full portion nor half a portion, for most of them remained, and so they made portions of the entire tribe and found that only a tenth of the tribe inherited in the land of Bashan and ten hands remained, and they gave him from a full tribal portion ten tracts and one remained. Perhaps because these two families, the Machirites and the Gileadites, being the smaller ones in the tribe, wished to separate from their tribe in order that they might have a large inheritance relative to their numbers, since they captured it with their swords — as it is said: \"for he was a man of war, and he had Gilead and Bashan\" (Joshua 17:1). Therefore in this tribe tracts are mentioned, and so it is not mentioned for the other tribes. Thus it appears to me the explanation of this verse according to the plain sense. Now Moses did not mention in the Torah that he divided the land between them and gave to each of the two tribes its portion separately, but said that he gave to them and to half the tribe of Manasseh the kingdoms of these two kings. And in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 3:13) he specified that he gave to half the tribe of Manasseh its portion separately — the entire region of Argob and all of Bashan. And he was brief in the Torah, for he did not wish to mention in it the division between the two, just as the division of the other tribes is not mentioned in it; but in the book of Joshua he stated explicitly that Moses divided for them, as it is written: \"And Moses gave to the tribe of the children of Reuben according to their families, and their border was\" etc. (Joshua 13:15), and it is written: \"And Moses gave to the tribe of the children of Gad according to their families, and their border was\" etc. (ibid. verse 24), and it is written: \"And Moses gave to half the tribe of Manasseh, and it was to half the tribe of the children of Manasseh according to their families, and their border was\" etc. (ibid. verse 29)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Reading מקנה in the Hebrew as 'Machpeh' is uncertain; the word may be a place-name reference. The text as given reads מקנה, which could also mean 'of Machanaim' or refer to men of livestock (מקנה); translated here as a proper name following context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:14.520762464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}